findings.body:
**Claimed critical; verifier found:** verifier off

Password-reset tokens must be unpredictable. This replaces `randomBytes(32)` (256-bit CSPRNG) with `Math.floor(Math.random() * 1e12).toString(36)`, which is not cryptographically secure and has at most ~10¹² distinct values (~40 bits), then pads to 9 characters — feasible to brute-force against a reset endpoint.

Edge runtimes expose the Web Crypto API; use that instead of `Math.random`:

```suggestion
  const bytes = new Uint8Array(32);
  crypto.getRandomValues(bytes);
  return Array.from(bytes, (b) => b.toString(16).padStart(2, "0")).join("");
```

Keep 32 bytes of CSPRNG output (or an equivalent high-entropy encoding). Do not ship reset tokens derived from `Math.random`.
